{"id":"data-averages-spread","title":"Data science: averages and spread","subject":"Data science","label":"Data science","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":168,"duration":168.27,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":497,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"Two classes","start":0,"end":14.015},{"id":"explain","title":"Core idea","start":14.015,"end":41.765},{"id":"explain2","title":"Spread","start":41.765,"end":78.435},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":78.435,"end":107.275},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":107.275,"end":115.215},{"id":"answer","title":"Answer","start":115.215,"end":121.685},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":121.685,"end":143.815},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap","start":143.815,"end":168.27}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":4.1,"text":"I'm Kora. Imagine two classes both score a mean of sixty.","w":[0.54,0.7,1.48,1.94,2.16,2.66,2.92,3.24,3.32,3.54,3.68]},{"s":0,"a":4.4,"b":8.77,"text":"One class is clustered near sixty. The other runs from twenty to a hundred.","w":[4.52,4.72,5.2,5.36,5.84,6.08,6.96,7.1,7.34,7.62,7.84,8.22,8.38,8.46]},{"s":0,"a":9.07,"b":13.59,"text":"Which average should you trust, and what else do you need? This lesson shows you.","w":[9.12,9.54,9.82,10.02,10.18,11.02,11.16,11.4,11.6,11.72,11.84,12.54,12.78,13.12,13.4]},{"s":1,"a":14.44,"b":22.16,"text":"An average is one number that stands for a whole set of values. The mean is the sum of the values divided by how many values there are.","w":[14.5,14.76,15.18,15.42,15.62,15.9,16.1,16.48,16.64,16.76,17.12,17.34,17.42,18.48,18.66,18.98,19.1,19.28,19.64,19.74,19.86,20.32,20.74,20.94,21.1,21.32,21.72,22]},{"s":1,"a":22.46,"b":28.68,"text":"For example, the mean of two and four is three, because two plus four is six, and six divided by two is three.","w":[22.48,22.64,23.62,23.74,24,24.14,24.42,24.56,24.82,24.94,25.64,25.92,26.12,26.4,26.64,26.76,27.16,27.34,27.56,27.94,28.08,28.28,28.38]},{"s":1,"a":28.98,"b":36.08,"text":"The median is the middle value after you put the values in order. With an even count, take the mean of the two middle values.","w":[29,29.16,29.7,29.82,29.94,30.22,30.78,31,31.18,31.36,31.48,31.9,32.04,32.9,33.08,33.22,33.48,34.12,34.34,34.48,34.78,34.9,35.02,35.22,35.48]},{"s":1,"a":36.38,"b":41.34,"text":"The mode is the value that appears most often. Always say which average you mean.","w":[36.42,36.58,36.98,37.1,37.24,37.58,37.74,38.22,38.6,39.5,39.84,40.16,40.52,40.84,40.98]},{"s":2,"a":42.19,"b":47.94,"text":"Spread says how far apart the values are. The range is the largest value minus the smallest.","w":[42.26,42.66,42.96,43.2,43.44,43.76,43.9,44.36,45.14,45.3,45.7,45.84,45.98,46.46,46.86,47.24,47.36]},{"s":2,"a":48.24,"b":54.27,"text":"Data scientists also use standard deviation. It measures the typical distance of the values from the mean.","w":[48.24,48.58,49.34,49.72,49.9,50.32,51.44,51.56,51.98,52.12,52.56,52.98,53.08,53.18,53.6,53.78,53.9]},{"s":2,"a":54.57,"b":59.84,"text":"An outlier is a value far from the rest. It pulls the mean strongly but hardly moves the median.","w":[54.64,54.82,55.36,55.5,55.58,56,56.28,56.44,56.56,57.26,57.38,57.72,57.84,58.08,58.58,58.78,59.14,59.36,59.48]},{"s":2,"a":60.14,"b":66.41,"text":"So for incomes or house prices, the median is the fairer average. Always give an average with a measure of spread.","w":[60.22,60.5,60.72,61.16,61.3,61.56,62.28,62.42,62.92,63.04,63.18,63.62,64.4,64.7,64.86,65.04,65.36,65.54,65.62,65.92,66.02]},{"s":2,"a":66.71,"b":73.19,"text":"Two classes can share a mean of sixty, while one has everybody near sixty and the other has scores from twenty to a hundred.","w":[66.72,66.92,67.4,67.58,67.84,67.9,68.14,68.32,69.06,69.38,69.54,69.74,70.16,70.42,71.16,71.26,71.4,71.6,71.78,72.16,72.36,72.66,72.8,72.88]},{"s":2,"a":73.49,"b":78.01,"text":"The mean uses every value, so one extreme value can change it a lot.","w":[73.52,73.7,74.12,74.46,74.7,75.58,75.88,76.02,76.54,76.96,77.16,77.46,77.54,77.62]},{"s":3,"a":78.86,"b":84.43,"text":"Take the values two, four, four, five and ten. Their sum is twenty-five, so the mean is five.","w":[78.86,79.16,79.39,79.81,80.1,80.46,80.82,81.11,81.35,81.65,82,82.24,82.42,83.19,83.36,83.6,83.9,84.07]},{"s":3,"a":84.73,"b":89.73,"text":"In order, the middle value is four, so the median is four. The mode is four.","w":[84.82,85.02,85.36,85.54,85.8,86.22,86.42,87.24,87.4,87.52,87.94,88.12,88.88,89,89.3,89.44]},{"s":3,"a":90.03,"b":94.9,"text":"The range is ten minus two, which is eight. Now change the ten to one hundred.","w":[90.06,90.22,90.66,90.84,91.16,91.56,92.26,92.48,92.7,93.36,93.62,93.96,94.08,94.32,94.48,94.62]},{"s":3,"a":95.2,"b":101.93,"text":"The mean jumps to twenty-three, but the median stays four. One value changed, and the mean more than quadrupled.","w":[95.2,95.44,95.74,96.09,96.27,97.11,97.34,97.58,98,98.36,98.71,98.95,99.31,99.85,100.08,100.32,100.62,100.92,101.22]},{"s":3,"a":102.23,"b":106.85,"text":"That is the power of an outlier, and why one number alone can mislead you.","w":[102.26,102.52,102.64,102.8,103.26,103.36,103.5,104.52,104.66,104.98,105.16,105.48,105.94,106.14,106.64]},{"s":4,"a":107.7,"b":112.35,"text":"Quick check. Find the mean and the median of three, seven, seven, nine and fourteen.","w":[107.76,107.98,108.74,109.04,109.18,109.62,109.72,109.84,110.22,110.34,110.7,111.12,111.48,111.78,111.9]},{"s":4,"a":112.65,"b":114.79,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[112.7,113,113.3,113.44,113.72,114.28,114.44]},{"s":5,"a":115.64,"b":121.26,"text":"The sum is forty, so the mean is eight; the median is seven because it is the middle value in order.","w":[115.66,115.86,116.1,116.28,116.88,117.06,117.18,117.44,117.66,118.26,118.42,118.84,119,119.36,119.68,119.78,119.9,120.04,120.32,120.74,120.94]},{"s":6,"a":122.11,"b":128.57,"text":"Mistake one: forgetting to put the values in order before finding the median. Avoid it by sorting them first, every time.","w":[122.16,122.64,123.2,123.58,123.68,123.84,123.94,124.36,124.52,124.74,125.08,125.38,125.48,126.38,126.72,126.82,126.98,127.3,127.5,128.1,128.34]},{"s":6,"a":128.87,"b":135.5,"text":"Mistake two: dividing by the wrong count when finding the mean. Count the values carefully, then divide.","w":[128.94,129.38,130.04,130.54,130.68,130.82,131.14,131.44,131.64,132,132.1,132.94,133.2,133.3,133.74,134.76,135]},{"s":6,"a":135.8,"b":143.39,"text":"Mistake three: saying the average without naming which one, or ignoring outliers and spread. Name your average, and pair it with a range.","w":[135.86,136.3,136.94,137.24,137.44,137.74,138.06,138.34,138.64,139.12,139.26,139.72,140.22,140.36,141.32,141.54,141.8,142.2,142.36,142.6,142.72,142.9,142.98]},{"s":7,"a":144.24,"b":148.81,"text":"The mean, median and mode are different averages. Name the one you use.","w":[144.3,144.48,145.02,145.52,145.66,146.02,146.18,146.58,147.64,147.88,148.06,148.2,148.46]},{"s":7,"a":149.11,"b":155.18,"text":"The range and standard deviation describe spread. An outlier pulls the mean far more than the median.","w":[149.12,149.3,149.72,149.88,150.3,150.88,151.36,152.28,152.58,153.14,153.46,153.56,153.92,154.28,154.56,154.72,154.82]},{"s":7,"a":155.48,"b":158.02,"text":"Always report an average with a measure of spread.","w":[155.56,155.94,156.32,156.5,156.84,157.02,157.12,157.52,157.66]},{"s":7,"a":158.32,"b":163.05,"text":"Top tip: for skewed data like incomes, use the median and give the range too.","w":[158.36,158.7,159.26,159.44,159.82,160.12,160.36,161.32,161.48,161.6,162.06,162.18,162.34,162.48,162.76]},{"s":7,"a":163.35,"b":167.67,"text":"Well done. Keep practising with Knowzone, and these ideas will become simple.","w":[163.42,163.64,164.36,164.6,165.16,165.32,166,166.12,166.38,166.78,166.92,167.28]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"Same mean, different data","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Two classes: mean = 60"},{"at":4.3,"kind":"text","text":"One clustered near 60; one 20–100"},{"at":8.97,"kind":"text","text":"Which average? What else?"}]},{"at":14.19,"heading":"Mean, median, mode","items":[{"at":14.34,"kind":"text","text":"Average: one number for a set"},{"at":14.34,"kind":"term","text":"Mean","detail":"sum of values ÷ how many values"},{"at":22.36,"kind":"formula","text":"Mean(2,4) = (2+4)÷2 = 3"},{"at":28.88,"kind":"term","text":"Median","detail":"middle value after ordering; even count: mean of two middle"},{"at":36.28,"kind":"term","text":"Mode","detail":"value appearing most often; name your average"}]},{"at":41.94,"heading":"Spread and outliers","items":[{"at":42.09,"kind":"term","text":"Range","detail":"largest value − smallest value"},{"at":48.14,"kind":"term","text":"Standard deviation","detail":"typical distance of values from the mean"},{"at":54.47,"kind":"term","text":"Outlier","detail":"value far from the rest; pulls mean, hardly median"},{"at":60.04,"kind":"text","text":"Median fairer for incomes; give average + spread"},{"at":73.39,"kind":"text","text":"Mean uses every value; extremes move it"}]},{"at":78.61,"heading":"Worked example","items":[{"at":78.76,"kind":"formula","text":"2+4+4+5+10 = 25 → mean = 5"},{"at":84.63,"kind":"formula","text":"Median = 4; mode = 4"},{"at":89.93,"kind":"formula","text":"Range = 10 − 2 = 8"},{"at":95.1,"kind":"formula","text":"10→100: mean = 23; median = 4"},{"at":102.13,"kind":"text","text":"Outlier: mean more than quadrupled"}]},{"at":107.28,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":107.28,"kind":"question","text":"Find the mean and median of 3, 7, 7, 9 and 14."}]},{"at":115.39,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":115.54,"kind":"formula","text":"Sum = 40 → mean = 8"},{"at":115.54,"kind":"formula","text":"Median = 7"}]},{"at":121.86,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":122.01,"kind":"wrong","text":"Finding median before sorting"},{"at":128.77,"kind":"wrong","text":"Dividing by wrong count for mean"},{"at":135.7,"kind":"wrong","text":"Saying 'average' without naming it"}]},{"at":143.99,"heading":"Recap","items":[{"at":144.14,"kind":"text","text":"Mean, median, mode: name your average"},{"at":149.01,"kind":"text","text":"Range and standard deviation describe spread"},{"at":149.01,"kind":"text","text":"Outlier pulls mean more than median"},{"at":155.38,"kind":"text","text":"Always report average + spread"},{"at":158.22,"kind":"tip","text":"For skewed data like incomes, use median and range"}]}],"check":{"at":115.04,"seconds":10,"question":"Find the mean and median of 3, 7, 7, 9 and 14.","answer":"Mean = 8; median = 7."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}